course: dictionary
lesson: 1245
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. Context, meaning the words around the word, tells you which meaning the writer wants. «Malik's aunt already has three kids, so Malik shares a bunk bed with his cousin Dre.» Here a bunk is one of two beds built one above the other.
explain: The word bunk can also mean a narrow bed fixed to a wall, like the ones on a ship or a train. The Rat sees «little sleeping-bunks in the wall» in Mole's home. Ask yourself: is this a bed, or is it an action? Then try the meaning in the sentence to check it fits.
explain: Bunk can also be a verb, which is an action word. It is informal, so you will hear it in chatty speech. The phrase «bunk off» is British and informal too, and it means to stay away from school or work without permission. Chatty words are often shorter, so check the sentence carefully.
explain: Context can also warn you about safety. «A fall from a window or a top bunk hurts badly» shows that a top bunk is high up. That is why small children should not sleep in the top bunk, and why a bunk should have a rail so no one falls out.
fact: A bunk is a narrow bed fixed to a wall, such as on a ship or a train.
fact: A bunk bed is one of two beds built one above the other.
fact: As a verb, bunk means to sleep somewhere, often with others. It is informal.
fact: «Bunk off» is British informal slang. It means to stay away from school or work without permission.
fact: Safety tip: small children should not sleep in the top bunk, and bunks should have a rail so no one falls out.
exercise: «Malik shares a bunk bed with his cousin Dre.» What is a bunk bed? | A table that folds up | A kind of cooker | A bed that folds away into the wall | *One of two beds built one above the other
exercise: Sam says he will bunk off school tomorrow. What does bunk off mean? | Go to school early | *Stay away from school without permission | Clean the classroom | Sleep on a ship
exercise: At camp, everyone may have to bunk with others. What does bunk mean here? | To pay for a ticket | To cook a meal | To jump up and down | *To sleep somewhere, often with others
test: «The Rat admires the little sleeping-bunks in the wall.» What is a sleeping-bunk here? | *A narrow bed fixed to the wall | A bird-cage | A stove | A bookshelf
test: Which phrase means to stay away from school without permission? | *bunk off school | top bunk | bunk bed | bottom bunk
exam: «In Liverpool, twins Kieron and Kaden have bunk beds; every night Kaden kicks the top bunk from below.» What is the top bunk? | The bed nearest the floor | A cupboard for clothes | *The upper one of two beds built one above the other | A rail that stops falls
